1
00:00:02,260 --> 00:00:03,960
Anytime this year, Ben.

2
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:05,560
The lock is messed up.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:06,700
You're messed up.

4
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:09,140
It's been a lovely birthday.

5
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:10,280
Mike, don't ruin it.

6
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:11,400
No, I'm not thinking of me.

7
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:14,200
I'm thinking of poor old Dana who has to carry all your presents.

8
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:17,520
How thoughtful. There.

9
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:23,560
Oh, this has been a lot of fun.

10
00:00:23,940 --> 00:00:25,740
Well, I just want to thank all of you for the...

11
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:27,300
Maggie, can I just put these down first?

12
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:29,480
Oh, sure. Sorry.

13
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:34,480
Hey. I just want to thank all of you for your wonderful gifts.

14
00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:36,220
Except Mike, of course.

15
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:40,740
What? Next time you buy perfume, don't buy it at a bowling alley.

16
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:44,900
Excuse me, why isn't the light on in the living room?

17
00:00:45,220 --> 00:00:45,680
I don't know.

18
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:46,500
Maybe it burned up.

19
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:50,140
Uh -huh. And you had nothing to do with it?

20
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:53,560
What? Carol? What?

21
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:55,960
Mike. Hey, I didn't do it.

22
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,720
You guys are throwing me a surprise party?

23
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:04,840
Uh, no. Don't ever play poker, Jason.

24
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:06,080
You're a terrible bluffer.

25
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:07,600
Uh, no, Mom.

26
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:08,560
There's no party.

27
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:11,600
See, we discussed it, and we decided that it would be cheaper.

28
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:17,300
Easier if we just took you out to dinner.

29
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:23,680
Right. Now, Maggie, all these years, you've always said how you hate surprise

30
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:25,940
parties. Yes, but underneath, you knew what I would do.

31
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:29,980
and you ignored what I was saying, and that's why I love you.

32
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:31,080
What are you saying?

33
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:33,140
I'm ready for my party.

34
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:38,380
Boy, is it dark in here, honey.

35
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:42,040
You better turn on a light before I trip over something or somebody.

36
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:46,380
Oh. Oh, my.

37
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:54,820
God. You know what?

38
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:57,020
Someone's been here.

39
00:01:58,300 --> 00:02:00,620
Show me that smile again.

40
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:02,600
Show me that smile.

41
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:06,640
Don't waste another minute on your crying.

42
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:08,820
We're nowhere near the end.

43
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:11,020
We're nowhere near the best.

44
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:13,020
He's ready to begin.

45
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:19,150
Oh, oh. As long as we got each other,

46
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:24,240
we got the world spinning right in our hands.

47
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:26,000
Baby, you and me.

48
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:34,560
We gotta be the luckiest dreamers who never quit dreaming As long as we keep on

49
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:44,820
giving We can take anything that comes our way Baby, let us shine all the time We

50
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:50,540
got each other sharing the laughter and love

51
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:02,300
We've been robbed!

52
00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:04,580
Yeah. Everybody just take it easy.

53
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:05,900
First thing, we'll call the police.

54
00:03:06,640 --> 00:03:08,900
Jason, look at my knickknacks.

55
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:12,260
Well, those thieves didn't miss a thing.

56
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:13,080
They even got the phone.

57
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:15,380
Why would someone rob us?

58
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:16,620
We're the severs.

59
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:19,120
Come on, we used the one in the kitchen.

60
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:23,060
Oh, good. At least they didn't take that phone.

61
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:23,980
Dad, Dad, don't touch it.

62
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:25,340
There could be fingerprints anywhere, okay?

63
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:25,980
I like to put my fingerprints in the kitchen.

64
00:03:25,980 --> 00:03:27,380
My fingerprints somewhere.

65
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:29,980
Right now, everybody, I just want you all to just calm down, okay?

66
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,220
Calm down, Dad, we've been invaded here.

67
00:03:32,380 --> 00:03:34,060
Yeah, they could've taken...

68
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:37,940
My stuff! Ah!

69
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:41,280
Okay. Okay. Oh, mine.

70
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:43,460
Oh. Ah, excuse me.

71
00:03:44,180 --> 00:03:45,640
Would you look at this?

72
00:03:45,740 --> 00:03:47,620
The thieves actually had a cup of coffee.

73
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:51,220
Hello, yes, I want to report a burglary.

74
00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:52,740
Yeah, my name is...

75
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:54,260
Jason! I know that.

76
00:03:55,980 --> 00:03:57,780
It's still hot!

77
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:10,220
What's that noise?

78
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:15,180
What noise? It sounded like someone walking through the bushes with a big knife and

79
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:16,280
a gun and they...

80
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:17,440
It's your imagination, Carol.

81
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:19,860
Jason, it has been over an hour.

82
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:20,880
Where are the police?

83
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:21,860
This just figures.

84
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:24,000
There's only cops around when you don't need them.

85
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:28,080
Like when? You know, like when you're speeding a little bit, or sneaking into a

86
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:29,880
drive -in, or borrowing a street sign.

87
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:34,320
These are just random examples, Dad.

88
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:37,220
Where are those clowns anyway?

89
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:41,340
Ah! Wait, wait, wait!

90
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:43,720
The police are clowns.

91
00:04:44,860 --> 00:04:45,600
You're the savers?

92
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:47,220
Yes, who are you?

93
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:52,480
I'm a happy birthday clown, happy birthday clown.

94
00:04:52,940 --> 00:04:54,420
Happy, happy, happy, happy birthday.

95
00:04:54,420 --> 00:04:56,600
Hey, hey, hey, hey, hey, hey.

96
00:04:57,020 --> 00:04:58,220
Well, that was pretty rude.

97
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:02,060
Well, you were supposed to be here at 9 o 'clock.

98
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:04,400
And you were supposed to be Humphrey Bogart.

99
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:06,860
Oh, wow, you're kidding.

100
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:08,960
I feel terrible.

101
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:13,040
What a mix -up.

102
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:14,580
You must think I'm a total bozo.

103
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:18,780
Thank you anyway.

104
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:21,720
As long as I'm here, you want me to make you a balloon, Rhino?

105
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:24,840
Less than anything in the world.

106
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:30,880
Oh, Jason, robbed on my birthday.

107
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,240
Jason, it just doesn't seem fair.

108
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:36,700
Yeah. I wanted you to remember this birthday forever.

109
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:38,420
I wish you well, Dad.

110
00:05:41,260 --> 00:05:44,020
What are you people doing to me?

111
00:05:44,340 --> 00:05:45,740
Excuse me, are you the Sievers?

112
00:05:46,020 --> 00:05:48,520
Yes, yes. Officer Wright, Long Island PD.

113
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:49,620
You reported a burglary?

114
00:05:50,140 --> 00:05:51,120
Uh, wait a minute.

115
00:05:51,120 --> 00:05:52,160
But you got some ID?

116
00:05:54,920 --> 00:05:56,400
You think he's my jammie son?

117
00:05:57,920 --> 00:05:59,240
Mike. Come on, dad.

118
00:05:59,240 --> 00:06:00,920
You could have rented that from a costume shop.

119
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:03,380
The man is a policeman, Mike, OK?

120
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:06,280
Thank you. Do you have some ID?

121
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:07,960
No, it's OK.

122
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:08,880
No, we'll trust you.

123
00:06:09,300 --> 00:06:11,360
So how about we go inside so I can take down your report?

124
00:06:11,820 --> 00:06:14,180
Well, we're not even sure the burglars have left yet.

125
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:18,700
Mr. Siever, it's been an hour.

126
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:21,580
You really think he's hanging around in there taking a bubble bath?

127
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:25,600
Good thinking. Okay, everybody, come on, let's go.

128
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:33,020
Jason, I am still scared.

129
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:35,400
Maggie, look at the size of this guy.

130
00:06:35,860 --> 00:06:36,740
I feel safe.

131
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:44,000
You know, my grandpa's a cop, so I know all about him.

132
00:06:44,140 --> 00:06:45,040
Well, watch my step.

133
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:46,820
He's not black, though.

134
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:58,120
Nobody's perfect. Excuse me.

135
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:02,920
Oh, right. What a shame.

136
00:07:04,340 --> 00:07:07,140
We reached right through here and popped the front door.

137
00:07:07,620 --> 00:07:08,180
Hey, come on.

138
00:07:08,200 --> 00:07:08,940
You're touching the glass.

139
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:10,120
How are you supposed to take fingerprints?

140
00:07:10,460 --> 00:07:13,040
We don't have the manpower to take prints in a minor crime.

141
00:07:13,880 --> 00:07:16,740
Minor? Minor? Look what they did to my house!

142
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:18,520
Well, no one was hurt.

143
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:21,020
And that's our fault.

144
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:24,700
Look, if you don't take fingerprints, how are you supposed to put out an APB?

145
00:07:25,160 --> 00:07:30,560
We don't. Well, not exactly Miami Vice now, are we?

146
00:07:33,420 --> 00:07:35,660
And you're not exactly the Cosby show either.

147
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,380
Uh, excuse me, but would it ruin any evidence if I go to the bathroom?

148
00:07:46,700 --> 00:07:48,520
Because if not, we're gonna have a major problem.

149
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:49,940
No, that should be okay.

150
00:07:50,080 --> 00:07:51,760
Thanks. Hello, officer.

151
00:07:51,780 --> 00:07:53,240
If you don't take prints, what do you do?

152
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:56,480
Well, first I look for any hard evidence.

153
00:07:56,680 --> 00:07:58,600
You know, burglar tools, footprints.

154
00:07:58,980 --> 00:07:59,680
Point of entry.

155
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:03,820
Right. Then we fill out a report listing all your stolen items.

156
00:08:04,020 --> 00:08:05,720
You'll need that for your insurance company.

157
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:07,800
Then I go to my next burglary call.

158
00:08:08,680 --> 00:08:10,260
Is that how your grandpa would handle it?

159
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:16,180
No. He'd single -handedly capture the bad guys, take their guns, and be invited to

160
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:18,500
the mayor's for lunch and a hoist if you'll.

161
00:08:20,780 --> 00:08:23,120
Wow. Well, say what you want.

162
00:08:23,180 --> 00:08:25,400
I don't understand how this is a minor crime.

163
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:26,960
Our home has been violated.

164
00:08:27,180 --> 00:08:28,300
Personal things have been taken.

165
00:08:28,980 --> 00:08:30,600
And it's my birthday.

166
00:08:31,880 --> 00:08:33,080
Hey, happy birthday.

167
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:38,520
Now, should we wait before we file the insurance claim, give you guys a chance to

168
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:39,120
find our stuff?

169
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:41,180
Heh, heh, heh, heh.

170
00:08:42,340 --> 00:08:43,340
Is that a no?

171
00:08:43,480 --> 00:08:44,600
Heh, heh, heh.

172
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:49,620
Jason, look at this.

173
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:50,760
Just a second, honey.

174
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:52,040
This is important.

175
00:08:52,180 --> 00:08:54,460
What? My secret recipe chicken soup.

176
00:08:56,480 --> 00:08:57,820
It's gone too.

177
00:08:58,400 --> 00:09:00,120
Well, yeah, maybe just one of the kids, but...

178
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:00,780
No, no, no.

179
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:03,120
Ask them. Can you believe this?

180
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:05,640
Honey, we're missing a lot of things.

181
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:07,640
What's so important about your chicken soup?

182
00:09:08,620 --> 00:09:10,420
I didn't make it for them.

183
00:09:10,420 --> 00:09:11,600
I made it for us.

184
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:13,360
Well, that just about covers it.

185
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:15,360
Add chicken soup to that list.

186
00:09:15,560 --> 00:09:17,620
What? Or is that too minor?

187
00:09:19,100 --> 00:09:20,280
I'd do it.

188
00:09:21,160 --> 00:09:23,020
Chicken soup. With noodles?

189
00:09:23,420 --> 00:09:26,240
Got it. If there's any additional missing items, here's my card.

190
00:09:27,080 --> 00:09:28,600
Wanna get a cigarette and a cup of coffee?

191
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:31,080
Mmm. No, not tonight.

192
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:33,360
Too busy. Oh, come on.

193
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:34,660
You don't have to go yet.

194
00:09:34,760 --> 00:09:38,380
Stay. Eat. Mom, make him a cake.

195
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:40,820
Can't stay, little buddy.

196
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:44,580
But who's gonna protect us when the bad guys get back?

197
00:09:44,780 --> 00:09:45,980
Hey, hey, hey.

198
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:48,900
Don't worry. They won't come back tonight.

199
00:09:53,640 --> 00:10:00,060
Tonight? And the silver candlesticks are gone, too.

200
00:10:00,220 --> 00:10:01,280
That's about it.

201
00:10:01,580 --> 00:10:02,620
Aren't you forgetting something?

202
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:06,180
What? Well, that sweater that your mother knitted for me.

203
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:08,220
With the sleeve that's five feet long.

204
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:10,920
It's right in your hand.

205
00:10:11,040 --> 00:10:13,680
Well, you know that and I know that, but your mother doesn't know it.

206
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:18,160
My mother worked very hard on that sweater.

207
00:10:18,440 --> 00:10:20,680
That's why it's gonna be such a tragic love.

208
00:10:24,980 --> 00:10:28,880
Maggie? Honey, what is it?

209
00:10:30,100 --> 00:10:34,060
Hey. Oh, Jason, they...

210
00:10:34,060 --> 00:10:37,740
they came into my house, they touched our things, they went through everything.

211
00:10:38,580 --> 00:10:41,260
They know what size my underwear is.

212
00:10:42,580 --> 00:10:43,700
Well, we're okay.

213
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:44,980
Come on. Nobody was hurt.

214
00:10:45,260 --> 00:10:47,520
But what if they come back when we're here?

215
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:49,000
Why would they come back?

216
00:10:49,540 --> 00:10:51,420
Why did they come in the first place?

217
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:54,720
Hi. Hi, honey.

218
00:10:55,420 --> 00:10:57,060
Can I come sleep with you guys?

219
00:10:58,320 --> 00:11:07,640
Sure. Hey, nobody's getting my stuff.

220
00:11:09,300 --> 00:11:11,220
Uh, hi. Hi, sweetheart.

221
00:11:12,020 --> 00:11:15,740
Uh, listen, you know, I was wondering...

222
00:11:15,740 --> 00:11:17,340
Come on in.

223
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:23,760
Thanks. Hey. You scared too?

224
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:26,580
Me? Scared? Come on, man.

225
00:11:26,580 --> 00:11:31,200
I just, uh, I thought that you guys could use a little protection up here.

226
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:34,280
I don't buy that for a second.

227
00:11:34,960 --> 00:11:45,660
Hey. Mommy! Mommy!

228
00:11:45,820 --> 00:11:46,740
Mommy! Mommy! Mommy!

229
00:11:46,740 --> 00:11:48,100
Mommy! Mommy! Mommy!

230
00:11:55,040 --> 00:11:57,420
Mommy! Mommy! Mommy!

231
00:11:57,820 --> 00:11:58,020
Mommy! Mommy! Mommy!

232
00:11:58,220 --> 00:11:59,560
Mommy! Mommy! Mommy!

233
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:00,720
Mommy! Mommy! Mommy!

234
00:12:00,740 --> 00:12:00,880
Mommy! Mommy! Mommy!

235
00:12:00,900 --> 00:12:05,000
Mommy! Mommy! Mommy!

236
00:12:05,080 --> 00:12:07,760
Mommy! we should go explore our feelings about what's happened.

237
00:12:08,120 --> 00:12:09,400
I don't believe this.

238
00:12:09,820 --> 00:12:10,400
No, I mean it.

239
00:12:10,400 --> 00:12:11,540
Come on. Everybody up.

240
00:12:11,700 --> 00:12:14,440
Let's go. Conversation is just what the doctor ordered.

241
00:12:14,740 --> 00:12:15,840
I know. I'm the doctor.

242
00:12:16,140 --> 00:12:17,840
We don't have anything to talk about.

243
00:12:18,040 --> 00:12:19,180
Oh, your dad's right.

244
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:22,160
We could use some help falling asleep, and listening to your father could put us

245
00:12:22,160 --> 00:12:25,020
right out. Thank you.

246
00:12:25,380 --> 00:12:26,520
I didn't mean that.

247
00:12:26,540 --> 00:12:28,140
I don't know what I meant, but I didn't mean that.

248
00:12:29,340 --> 00:12:30,680
Okay. I meant it.

249
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:32,160
Come on. Let's go.

250
00:12:32,340 --> 00:12:34,280
Hey, Dad. And, uh, you want me to wake Ben up?

251
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:37,600
So we can find out why he's having trouble sleeping?

252
00:12:38,620 --> 00:12:40,000
Oh, good my girl.

253
00:12:44,180 --> 00:12:47,640
Well, since something like this has never happened to us before, it's important that

254
00:12:47,640 --> 00:12:50,960
we dig into our anxiety, examine our feelings.

255
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:53,680
We've got to go inward and downward.

256
00:12:55,280 --> 00:12:56,740
I know. I'll make coffee.

257
00:12:57,360 --> 00:12:58,560
That'll help us sleep.

258
00:12:58,880 --> 00:13:01,760
OK, decaf. I'm in no mood to argue, Jason.

259
00:13:06,280 --> 00:13:08,020
Okay, kids, come on.

260
00:13:08,220 --> 00:13:10,020
Let's talk about what's bothering us.

261
00:13:10,160 --> 00:13:13,360
I see no reason whatsoever to discuss this a moment longer.

262
00:13:14,060 --> 00:13:14,960
That's a good start.

263
00:13:16,080 --> 00:13:21,700
Mike? Mike? Oh, uh, right.

264
00:13:22,200 --> 00:13:24,000
But yeah, yeah, that's a real good start, Carol.

265
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,740
Let's get in touch for a bit with why you're so weird, all right?

266
00:13:27,740 --> 00:13:29,500
Hey. This is not good.

267
00:13:29,500 --> 00:13:32,380
I bet one of your scumble friends has something to do with this burglary.

268
00:13:32,600 --> 00:13:34,860
Hey, hey, don't talk about my friends like that, all right?

269
00:13:35,060 --> 00:13:37,420
Eddie and Boner are too stupid to think of something like this.

270
00:13:37,780 --> 00:13:39,140
Look at you two kids.

271
00:13:39,420 --> 00:13:40,400
Stinky, stinky, stinky!

272
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:45,000
Hey! What? See, this is good.

273
00:13:47,580 --> 00:13:51,520
See, the anger you two are feeling right now is a carryover from the burglary.

274
00:13:51,700 --> 00:13:54,080
And it's good to just let me finish.

275
00:13:54,800 --> 00:13:55,980
Here's a good one.

276
00:13:56,280 --> 00:13:59,180
I'm coming. Maggie?

277
00:14:04,280 --> 00:14:08,180
I'm coming. I'm coming.

278
00:14:08,180 --> 00:14:08,860
Honey, what is it?

279
00:14:09,100 --> 00:14:09,760
What is what?

280
00:14:10,380 --> 00:14:12,020
You came in here to make coffee.

281
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:13,880
You want me to make coffee?

282
00:14:13,920 --> 00:14:16,480
Jason, we're having a hard enough time getting to sleep as it is.

283
00:14:17,140 --> 00:14:21,140
Okay. Then let's just go in and sit down and talk about what's disturbing us.

284
00:14:21,220 --> 00:14:22,580
Oh, nothing's disturbing me.

285
00:14:22,580 --> 00:14:25,540
Just because I want to sterilize everything that scum touched.

286
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:27,540
I know. I...

287
00:14:27,540 --> 00:14:28,600
I mean, he had a cup of coffee.

288
00:14:28,660 --> 00:14:30,540
You want to drink out of the same coffee pot he did?

289
00:14:31,260 --> 00:14:32,840
Honey, I don't even want coffee.

290
00:14:33,780 --> 00:14:35,800
Well, then what did you ask me to make it for?

291
00:14:37,580 --> 00:14:42,460
Ah! Bill? Ah!

292
00:14:42,720 --> 00:14:52,300
Ah! Ben, why are you screaming?

293
00:14:52,900 --> 00:14:54,680
When I woke up, you guys were gone.

294
00:14:54,820 --> 00:14:56,480
I thought somebody stole you.

295
00:14:57,720 --> 00:14:59,100
Oh, grow up, Ben.

296
00:14:59,940 --> 00:15:01,280
Will you go easy on him, Mike?

297
00:15:01,740 --> 00:15:03,100
Ben, we're gonna be downstairs.

298
00:15:03,240 --> 00:15:05,440
If you want to join us, we're gonna be talking through our feelings.

299
00:15:06,040 --> 00:15:07,360
I don't have any feelings.

300
00:15:07,480 --> 00:15:09,300
I'm fine. You're fine?

301
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:12,220
I will be as soon as I change my shorts.

302
00:15:33,740 --> 00:15:35,800
Okay, Mike, we'll come back to you.

303
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:38,540
Ben, what about you?

304
00:15:38,820 --> 00:15:40,000
Shh. He's sleeping.

305
00:15:40,700 --> 00:15:43,800
Jason, this has been very helpful, and I feel much better.

306
00:15:44,260 --> 00:15:47,180
Now, if you'll excuse me, I have some cleaning to do.

307
00:15:48,640 --> 00:15:52,140
Dad, do you think the thieves are sitting around in the thief place exploring their

308
00:15:52,140 --> 00:15:55,900
emotions? Maggie, can you just sit down, please?

309
00:15:56,320 --> 00:15:58,120
I can clean while we talk, can't I?

310
00:15:58,400 --> 00:15:59,320
All right, sure.

311
00:16:00,020 --> 00:16:04,560
Whoo! Whoa. Maggie, could you turn that off?

312
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:06,780
What? Could you turn it off?

313
00:16:07,960 --> 00:16:09,980
I'm sorry. I couldn't hear you over the back here.

314
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:10,320
Come on, Queen.

315
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:12,220
You're okay, Ben.

316
00:16:12,280 --> 00:16:12,740
It's all right.

317
00:16:12,760 --> 00:16:13,920
You're safe. You're safe.

318
00:16:14,340 --> 00:16:17,220
Oh, good. I wish I could buy that.

319
00:16:18,020 --> 00:16:19,460
Are you saying we're not safe?

320
00:16:19,760 --> 00:16:20,720
Let's put it this way, Ben.

321
00:16:20,720 --> 00:16:24,140
If some big dude breaks into the house, you think Dad is gonna wrestle him to the

322
00:16:24,140 --> 00:16:26,980
ground? Or Mom.

323
00:16:28,700 --> 00:16:31,840
Who else thinks there's a possibility someone else will break in?

324
00:16:33,300 --> 00:16:34,300
Come on, be honest.

325
00:16:38,920 --> 00:16:42,540
Maggie? Well, you wanted honesty.

326
00:16:42,660 --> 00:16:43,920
See what happens when you ask for that?

327
00:16:43,960 --> 00:16:46,500
I was quite happy to stand here with my vacuum cleaner in line.

328
00:16:47,180 --> 00:16:48,120
No, I'm glad you didn't.

329
00:16:48,320 --> 00:16:49,000
This is good.

330
00:16:52,440 --> 00:16:55,880
Well, I mean, it's good that we're finally able to admit that the reason we can't

331
00:16:55,880 --> 00:16:57,100
sleep is we're afraid.

332
00:16:57,640 --> 00:16:58,420
All of us.

333
00:16:59,340 --> 00:17:00,640
You're afraid, too?

334
00:17:00,760 --> 00:17:03,580
Yes, Ben. You're a dad in everything.

335
00:17:05,320 --> 00:17:08,220
Dad, if you keep going on like this, this kid is gonna explode.

336
00:17:09,140 --> 00:17:12,320
Look, I don't mean to frighten anyone, but if we don't deal with this honestly,

337
00:17:12,420 --> 00:17:14,440
Ben's gonna be dragging that box to college.

338
00:17:15,920 --> 00:17:16,820
It's not heavy.

339
00:17:19,180 --> 00:17:20,440
Well, what should we do?

340
00:17:20,580 --> 00:17:21,920
Well, that's the question.

341
00:17:23,020 --> 00:17:25,100
Something terrible has just happened to us.

342
00:17:25,340 --> 00:17:26,240
What do we do?

343
00:17:26,680 --> 00:17:29,780
Dad, is this one of those questions that you always ask us that you already know the

344
00:17:29,780 --> 00:17:32,700
answer to? Well, I have some thoughts.

345
00:17:32,700 --> 00:17:33,860
I'd like to hear yours.

346
00:17:33,860 --> 00:17:37,440
I say we get a burglar alarm, the biggest and loudest.

347
00:17:37,660 --> 00:17:40,760
Do you honestly think Sylvester Stallone has a burglar alarm?

348
00:17:41,540 --> 00:17:43,940
No, his wife got that in the settlement.

349
00:17:45,720 --> 00:17:49,860
Look, my point is, the only way to protect yourself is with guns, lots of them.

350
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,340
Yeah! No. That's so stupid.

351
00:17:52,440 --> 00:17:53,500
You got a better idea?

352
00:17:53,580 --> 00:17:54,940
As a matter of fact, I do.

353
00:17:55,080 --> 00:17:55,960
All right, let's hear it.

354
00:17:56,160 --> 00:18:01,920
Okay. Well, ever since this has happened, I've been asking myself why the thieves

355
00:18:01,920 --> 00:18:03,220
come here. Why?

356
00:18:03,860 --> 00:18:04,660
To steal junk.

357
00:18:05,220 --> 00:18:07,240
Exactly. And they call her the genius?

358
00:18:09,260 --> 00:18:13,100
You can't steal someone's possessions if they don't have any, right?

359
00:18:13,700 --> 00:18:17,320
So the answer is, we get rid of all our possessions.

360
00:18:20,160 --> 00:18:23,260
Everybody who thinks that's the stupidest idea they ever heard, raise their hand.

361
00:18:24,780 --> 00:18:25,740
All right, what's it gonna be?

362
00:18:26,040 --> 00:18:26,980
Rifles or handguns?

363
00:18:27,240 --> 00:18:29,560
Neither. I will not have my children armed.

364
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:30,900
It's a rule I have.

365
00:18:31,040 --> 00:18:32,600
I'm sorry I didn't mention it before.

366
00:18:33,620 --> 00:18:35,480
Well, I'd like to explore Carol's idea.

367
00:18:36,380 --> 00:18:37,620
This shouldn't be a short trip.

368
00:18:40,340 --> 00:18:41,220
You know, I'm not a Carol.

369
00:18:41,280 --> 00:18:44,060
Are you saying that we should, uh, get rid of our furniture?

370
00:18:44,420 --> 00:18:47,120
Yes. Our appliances.

371
00:18:47,460 --> 00:18:50,080
Sure. Your hairdryer.

372
00:18:52,420 --> 00:18:58,220
Well, seeing in the proper perspective, a hairdryer is really nothing more than

373
00:18:58,220 --> 00:19:01,560
concentrated wind, which is free, and that's not a possession.

374
00:19:02,460 --> 00:19:03,940
See, it all works out.

375
00:19:05,280 --> 00:19:07,080
I wish I had a gun now.

376
00:19:09,560 --> 00:19:14,280
Jason, it would be helpful to limit this conversation to reasonable, rational

377
00:19:14,280 --> 00:19:16,100
solutions. Like what?

378
00:19:16,240 --> 00:19:19,360
Like putting our house up for sale and moving to a small island off the coast of

379
00:19:19,360 --> 00:19:23,180
Maine. Well, why not a small island off the coast of France?

380
00:19:23,520 --> 00:19:25,180
We don't speak French.

381
00:19:25,860 --> 00:19:31,860
Maggie. Look, I know we can't move.

382
00:19:33,260 --> 00:19:36,220
But I sure feel like going somewhere that's secure.

383
00:19:36,520 --> 00:19:40,420
All I want is for my kids to feel as safe as I did when I was a little girl.

384
00:19:41,480 --> 00:19:43,620
Maggie, your father was a policeman.

385
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:45,800
Nobody would rob a cop.

386
00:19:46,260 --> 00:19:48,680
Right, because everybody knows that cops have guns.

387
00:19:50,720 --> 00:19:52,460
Guns are not the answer, Mike.

388
00:19:53,220 --> 00:19:55,940
Besides, if we had a gun, that could have gotten stolen, too.

389
00:19:56,180 --> 00:20:00,200
And there's a distinct possibility that somebody could get accidentally shot while

390
00:20:00,200 --> 00:20:01,520
sneaking in here late.

391
00:20:02,840 --> 00:20:03,700
That's a point.

392
00:20:05,220 --> 00:20:06,300
So we all agree.

393
00:20:06,300 --> 00:20:07,360
We give everything away.

394
00:20:07,760 --> 00:20:11,420
Right. What? Your father doesn't mean that.

395
00:20:11,420 --> 00:20:13,300
He's trying to make a point.

396
00:20:14,540 --> 00:20:16,540
You are trying to make a point, aren't you?

397
00:20:16,980 --> 00:20:18,740
Well, I think Carol has a good idea.

398
00:20:19,520 --> 00:20:21,140
Why not get rid of all of our possessions?

399
00:20:22,040 --> 00:20:23,680
Yeah. Get rid of everything.

400
00:20:23,720 --> 00:20:24,920
That way, nobody will rob us.

401
00:20:24,920 --> 00:20:25,900
Right. We'll be safe.

402
00:20:26,060 --> 00:20:30,320
Exactly. We can sit here, happy and secure on a bare floor, naked.

403
00:20:32,020 --> 00:20:35,720
Not me. Well, we'll save some rags for clothes.

404
00:20:36,160 --> 00:20:37,120
No brand names.

405
00:20:37,500 --> 00:20:41,480
Fine. Fine, but then what happens one day when somebody comes to the door?

406
00:20:41,580 --> 00:20:42,860
I forgot, we gave away the door.

407
00:20:43,220 --> 00:20:46,580
Somebody comes to where the door used to be, and they say, well, I notice you're all

408
00:20:46,580 --> 00:20:49,540
happy and secure in here, so why don't I just take that away too?

409
00:20:49,900 --> 00:20:51,180
Is this person a teacher?

410
00:20:52,780 --> 00:20:53,880
It doesn't matter.

411
00:20:54,240 --> 00:20:55,740
Dad, the question doesn't make sense.

412
00:20:55,740 --> 00:21:01,760
Why not? Well, if we're feeling safe and secure, no one can steal that.

413
00:21:03,460 --> 00:21:08,060
And isn't that exactly what they were letting the thieves do to us?

414
00:21:10,680 --> 00:21:12,320
Yeah. That's right.

415
00:21:13,320 --> 00:21:15,260
So what the heck are we supposed to do?

416
00:21:15,700 --> 00:21:17,260
We don't let them take it, Ben.

417
00:21:17,720 --> 00:21:19,020
We carry on.

418
00:21:19,680 --> 00:21:22,260
We do exactly what we would do every Saturday morning.

419
00:21:22,260 --> 00:21:24,940
Listen to you yell at Mike for sneaking in late.

420
00:21:26,300 --> 00:21:29,700
After that. We go out to breakfast.

421
00:21:29,900 --> 00:21:32,020
Right. Come on, what do you say?

422
00:21:32,480 --> 00:21:34,100
But who's gonna watch my stuff?

423
00:21:34,240 --> 00:21:36,500
Nobody, Ben. That's it.

424
00:21:36,560 --> 00:21:39,380
You see, either you own your stuff or your stuff owns you.

425
00:21:41,220 --> 00:21:44,980
Mike. I could eat.

426
00:21:46,120 --> 00:21:49,440
Carol? I feel like pigs in a blanket.

427
00:21:50,520 --> 00:21:51,460
Shut up, Michael.

428
00:21:58,980 --> 00:22:01,420
I knew you were making a point.

429
00:22:01,620 --> 00:22:03,260
I was sure of it all along.

430
00:22:03,460 --> 00:22:05,420
I never doubted you for a second.

431
00:22:06,400 --> 00:22:06,840
Okay come on.

432
00:22:06,880 --> 00:22:08,740
You've all got five minutes to change.

433
00:22:09,200 --> 00:22:13,420
Who needs Sylvester Stallone when we've got Jason Roland Sieber?

434
00:22:14,880 --> 00:22:18,320
Roland? Come on, get upstairs.

435
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:21,860
All right. Go ahead, Ben.

436
00:22:26,060 --> 00:22:28,640
Would it be all right if I just took my garbage pail, kids?

437
00:22:29,840 --> 00:22:31,300
Yeah. Come on, our secret.

438
00:23:42,300 --> 00:23:45,100
The End
